      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There is no clear consensus that definitively proves the cost-benefit trade-off is universally \"worth it,\" as different policy choices lead to varied outcomes. Proponents argue that the stability and improved health derived from safety nets drive better economic participation in the long run. Critics, however, point to specific policy designs, like high marginal tax rates on benefits, that directly create disincentives for re-entry into the workforce. Ultimately, whether the stability gain outweighs the participation dip is highly dependent on how the specific benefit structure is engineered.",
